FS.POLICY(5)                    File Formats                    FS.POLICY(5)

NAME
    fs.policy - who may reach which part of the filesystem

SYNOPSIS
    /etc/fs.policy

DESCRIPTION
    The filesystem policy says, for each login, which subtrees its session
    may reach and with which rights. fs_server reads it when it starts and
    compiles each login's grants into the one root capability that login's
    session is given; everything the session opens is reached through
    that capability, and the policy is applied to all of it.

    Logins do not open sessions yet: until the login handshake lands
    (roadmap 2.10, step 3(b)), the shell still authorises file access by
    the uid the kernel attests, and this file decides nothing at login.

FORMAT
    One grant per line, three fields separated by spaces or tabs:

        principal   path          rights

    principal  an account name; @all, every authenticated session;
               @admin, the administrator (uid 0); or @self, each account
               for its own home and its own /tmp.
    path       absolute, at most 8 components, each a valid file name.
               "~" means the account's home and is allowed only with
               @self, for a home of the form /home/NAME. "/tmp" with
               @self is the session's private /tmp (hier(7)).
    rights     comma-separated: read, write, append, exec, lookup,
               create, delete, setattr, or all.

    "#" starts a comment, and blank lines are ignored.

    A path named nowhere is reachable only through a grant on one of its
    ancestors. A directory on the way to a grant may be walked through and
    shows only the names that lead to grants; any other name in it is
    absent, not refused. Rights only add up: a line deeper in the tree
    never narrows what a line above it gave.

STRICT BY DESIGN
    Any line that does not parse makes the WHOLE file invalid; the lines
    that did parse are not used on their own. So are a file over 4096
    bytes or 64 grants, an unknown right, an unknown @group, a relative
    path, and the rights grant and snapshot, which are refused because a
    policy naming them would not do what its author meant.

    An invalid or missing file on an installed volume gives the
    administrator "/ all" and nobody anything else, so the administrator
    can log in and write one. A live boot uses the default below.

DEFAULT
    The live boot's policy, compiled into fs_server:

        @admin  /     all
        @all    /bin  read,lookup,exec
        @all    /lib  read,lookup
        @all    /usr  read,lookup,exec
        @self   ~     all
        @self   /tmp  read,write,lookup,create,delete,setattr

    /tmp is given without exec.

SECURITY
    Changing this file changes who may reach what, so only a login that
    holds write on /etc/fs.policy can do it; the default gives that to the
    administrator alone. Under "/ all" the administrator still cannot
    reach another session's private /tmp.

FILES
    /etc/fs.policy

SEE ALSO
    hier(7), capabilities(7), stat(1), chmod(1)
